  "surprise": "Canon layers (Canon-A + Canon-C per block, kernel 4, PyTorch default random init, 4,812,288 params) on 107-claude scored 456/489/465 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 107's 582/571/608: exact means 470.00 vs 587.00, -117.0, every seed down (paired -126/-82/-143), far below the registered -40..+45 range (central +10). Discard. Two surprises. (1) Cost: the 24 depthwise conv1d calls per step (plus cat/transpose) cost 20% of pretraining steps, 2627/2594/2582 vs 3264/3208/3277, seven times the predicted ~3%; RL sampling fell to 170-218 samples/s from 194-249, rounds took 303-365 s vs 266-322 s, totals 2004/1972/2084 s (under 2400). (2) The fit gap was small but the RL gap was large: final val loss 0.0842/0.0823/0.0846 vs 0.0822/0.0827/0.0836 and held-out greedy 0.972/0.977/0.974 vs 0.9815/0.977/0.9785, yet round-1 targets were 676/642/634 vs 722/689/679 with round-1 parse failures 10170/8039/7725 of 48,000 (21/17/16%; 107 averaged 15% over all passes), and the gap widened every round (r4 967/963/942 vs 1089/1031/1083; eval parse failures 11266/11039/10553 of 70,912; RL solved 957 mean vs 1068). The registered mechanism (cheap local mixing frees attention for long-range lookup and raises reasoning depth) is not supported: the model was slightly worse at everything, consistent with the random U(-0.5,0.5) four-tap mixing of the residual stream acting as fixed noise that a 2,600-step run cannot undo (AdamW moves a tap ~1e-3 per step, ~500 steps just to walk one from 0.5 to 0) and with the step loss. A zero-initialized canon (P44-style) on the branch input only would separate the mechanism from the init and cost penalties; not tried here.",
